## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design practical, measurable diagnostics for cross-region latency without code changes, focusing on network-layer factors and real-world tooling. It emphasizes data collection, hypothesis-driven testing, and concrete mitigations across DNS, CDN, and edge routing.

## Key Concepts
- Anycast and IXP ECMP behavior under load
- DNS latency vs HTTP latency as diagnostic signals
- TLS SNI routing at CDNs and edge caches
- CDN origin fetch queuing and caching strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple latency data parser (conceptual)
const fs = require('fs');
const rows = fs.readFileSync('latency.csv','utf8').trim().split('\n');
rows.slice(1).forEach(r => {
  const [region, dns, http] = r.split(',');
  console.log(region, parseFloat(dns), parseFloat(http));
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate this across regions to detect anomalies in near real-time?
- Which dashboards or alerts would you implement to surface ECMP churn vs CDN backlog quickly?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to diagnose cross-region telemetry issues in a service mesh with TLS, tenancy boundaries, and UDP paths.

## Key Concepts
- Edge load balancers TLS session resumption; per-tenant sidecar resource usage; MTU discovery and path fragmentation;
- NAT64 state handling; cross-region tunnel dynamics; observability across L4-L7 boundaries.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: quick script outline to surface TLS ticket counts from edge LB logs
const fs = require('fs');
const logs = fs.readFileSync('/var/log/edge/tls.log','utf8');
const tickets = logs.match(/TLS_ticket:(\d+)/g) || [];
console.log('Tickets seen:', tickets.length);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you safely roll out TLS ticket lifetime changes across regions?
- Which metrics differentiate sidecar starvation from path MTU fragmentation in this scenario?
